# Carrier-BSP env consumed by stone-provision-tegraflash.sh.
#
# Advantech MIC-742-AT — a Jetson AGX Thor module (P3834 CVM) on the Advantech
# P4071-class carrier. Advantech's own Flash SOP runs the STOCK NVIDIA profile
# (`l4t_initrd_flash.sh jetson-agx-thor-devkit internal`, i.e.
# p3834-0008-p4071-0000-nvme.conf), so the avocado-jetson-agx-thor MACHINE
# defaults are already the right baseline. Everything below is the carrier
# delta on top of it.
#
# BOTH module SKUs are supported by this one extension:
#
#   MIC-742-AT7A1   Jetson T5000, P3834-0008, 128GB
#   MIC-742-AT6A1   Jetson T4000, P3834-0000,  64GB
#
# Nothing here selects between them. initrd-flash.sh reads the module EEPROM
# before it prepares any binaries and retargets the seven module-specific
# flash files at whichever SKU it finds — including the BPMP DTB shipped in
# this directory, which is why both `-adv` copies are here. See
# meta-avocado-nvidia/docs/adding-a-jetson-carrier.md, section "Module SKU
# auto-detection (T264 / Thor)".
#
# Generic knob shape:
#   CARRIER_FV_<NAME>=value   → patches <NAME> in flashvars
#   CARRIER_ENV_<NAME>=value  → patches <NAME> in .env.initrd-flash
# Keys must start in column 0 — the rewrite loop strips the CARRIER_FV_/
# CARRIER_ENV_ prefix off the raw key, so a leading space makes it a no-op.
# See bsp-mic-733-ao5a1 for the Orin-era equivalent of this file, which does
# still pin its module SKU (T234's per-SKU file naming is irregular enough
# that the T264 substitution does not cover it).
#
# Derived by diffing Advantech's MIC-742_Thor_7.2_V1.0.0_SDK — the vendor ships
# one SDK for the MIC-74x family — against the stock NVIDIA
# Jetson_Linux_R39.2.0_aarch64 BSP, the SAME L4T release wrynose pins
# (meta-tegra tegra-binaries 39.2.0), so nothing here is a cross-release
# backport.

CARRIER_LABEL="Advantech MIC-742-AT (Jetson AGX Thor, P3834 on P4071 carrier)"

# --- Carrier files shadowing the MACHINE-baked stock names ------------------
#
# These three ship in this directory under their STOCK filenames and clobber
# the tegraflash-bsp copies when the overlay is flattened into the build dir.
# They are deliberately NOT renamed with an `-adv` suffix the way the Orin
# carriers do: meta-tegra generates thin `.dts` wrappers that #include the
# pinmux/gpio `.dtsi` BY NAME, so renaming the .dtsi would orphan it. Shadowing
# in place is the only form that works for those two, and `prod` follows suit
# for consistency. No CARRIER_FV_ override is needed for any of them.
#
#   tegra264-mb1-bct-pinmux-p3834-xxxx-p4071-0000.dtsi
#     61 changed pin blocks: CAN2/CAN3, eth0/eth3 MDIO+MDC, gen3/gen9 I2C,
#     PWM2/PWM3, DAP2/DAP6 (audio).
#   tegra264-mb1-bct-gpio-p3834-xxxx-p4071-0000.dtsi
#     Carrier boot GPIO defaults — the U257/U372 pin-header buffers and their
#     direction pins, internal USB VBUS enable, and the AON DI/DO inputs.
#   tegra264-mb1-bct-prod-p3834-xxxx-p4071-0000.dts
#     I2C1 drive-down strength fix (0x00001000 → 0x0000f000 on 0xac287014 /
#     0xac28701c). Without it MB1 cannot read the SoM EEPROM through the
#     carrier's 1.8V↔3.3V level shifter. Advantech cites NVIDIA bug 5459342.
#
# All three are p3834-xxxx — SOM-SKU independent by name, so the flash-time
# module retarget leaves them alone, and they serve the T4000 and the T5000
# equally.

# --- Kernel DT overlay ------------------------------------------------------
#
# Replaces the MACHINE's OVERLAY_DTB_FILE (tegra264-ftpm.dtbo) rather than
# adding to it. This is the entire kernel-DT carrier delta expressed as four
# fragments instead of a 256KB forked DTB, and Advantech's own T5000 and T4000
# DTBs carry it identically — see tegra264-mic-74x-carrier.dts for the
# per-fragment rationale.
#
# The replacement (not append) is deliberate and is the fTPM decision: the
# MIC-74x carries a discrete Infineon SLB9670 TPM 2.0 on SPI2 CS0, which the
# overlay binds. Applying tegra264-ftpm.dtbo as well would give Linux both the
# OP-TEE fTPM and the discrete part, racing for /dev/tpm0 vs /dev/tpm1 with no
# deterministic order — and anything sealing to a PCR (cryptsetup-var seals to
# PCR 7) would bind to whichever probed first. To go back to the fTPM on this
# board, set this to "tegra264-ftpm.dtbo" and drop kernel-module-tpm-tis-spi
# from avocado.yaml; the SATA/audio/sha fragments are then lost too, so prefer
# building a second dtbo if you actually want fTPM + carrier I/O.
CARRIER_FV_OVERLAY_DTB_FILE="tegra264-mic-74x-carrier.dtbo"

# --- PCIe C3 / SATA ---------------------------------------------------------
#
# The carrier's ASMedia ASM106x SATA AHCI controller sits on PCIe C3, which the
# devkit leaves disabled. Bringing it up takes THREE coordinated settings; miss
# any one and BPMP aborts at boot with
#
#   FATAL ERROR [FILE=platform/drivers/pcie/pcie4.c, ERR_UID=5213]:
#     PCIe(3): is enabled in DT without enabling it in UPHY DT
#
# which then cascades into "BPMP firmware is not ready" and a BL31 ASSERT at
# plat/nvidia/tegra/soc/t264/plat_setup.c:726 -- i.e. the board never reaches
# the kernel. The three settings are:
#
#   1. /uphy/uphy0-config = 6 in the BPMP DTB. This is the UPHY lane map that
#      includes PCIe C3; stock (and Advantech's own DTB on disk) ship 7.
#   2. /pcie/pcie@3 status = "okay" in the BPMP DTB.
#   3. pcie@a808440000 status = "okay" in the kernel DTB (overlay fragment@1).
#
# BOTH BPMP properties are baked into the carrier BPMP DTBs shipped here, so
# correctness does not depend on flash-time string handling. Advantech instead
# leaves uphy0-config at 7 on disk and relies on ODMDATA to patch it: tegraflash's
# tegraflash_update_bpmp_dtb() (bootloader/tegraflash_impl_t264.py) rewrites
# uphy0-config and the pcie@N status nodes in the BPMP DTB from --odmdata.
#
# That does NOT work on the avocado flash path today. initrd-flash.sh sources
# .env.initrd-flash WITHOUT exporting it, and sign_binaries() -- the function
# that actually generates and signs the flash images -- omits ODMDATA from its
# `VAR=value cmd` env prefix (create_rcm_boot_script() includes it; the bare
# `$ODMDATA` in the positional list is ignored, since the helper reads only
# three positionals). So $ODMDATA is empty inside tegra-flash-helper.sh, no
# --odmdata is passed, and the BPMP DTB is never patched. Verified on a
# MIC-743-AT7A1: ODMDATA was correctly rewritten in .env.initrd-flash and still
# had no effect, leaving uphy0-config at 7 and bricking the boot as above.
#
# ODMDATA is kept below anyway: it is Advantech's documented intent, it is
# harmless while inert, and if the initrd-flash export is ever fixed it sets
# exactly the two values already baked in -- so the two agree either way.
#
# UPHY_CONFIG is cleared to match Advantech. The MB1 UPHY BCT
# (tegra264-mb1-bct-uphy-lanes-p4071-0000.dts) maps UPHY0 lanes 4,5 to PCIe-C2
# and 6,7 to MPHY, with no C3 entry; the BPMP's uphy0-config is what assigns
# the C3 lanes, so leaving the static BCT in place would only conflict.
CARRIER_FV_UPHY_CONFIG=""
CARRIER_ENV_ODMDATA="uphy0-config-6,pcie@3_status=okay"

# BPMP DTB — Advantech's, renamed with an `-adv` suffix so its carrier origin
# is explicit against the stock name the MACHINE bakes, with uphy0-config
# additionally baked to 6 (see above). Delta from the stock
# tegra264-bpmp-3834-<sku>-4071-xxxx.dtb is exactly two properties, on BOTH:
#   /pcie/pcie@3 status  disabled -> okay   (Advantech's change, verbatim)
#   /uphy/uphy0-config   0x07     -> 0x06   (baked here; Advantech leaves this
#                                            to ODMDATA, which does not land)
#
# Set to the T5000 name because that is what the MACHINE bakes. On a T4000 the
# flash-time module retarget rewrites the `3834-0008` token to `3834-0000` and
# picks up the other file in this directory; both are shipped for that reason.
CARRIER_FV_BPFDTB_FILE="tegra264-bpmp-3834-0008-4071-xxxx-adv.dtb"

# --- Module gate ------------------------------------------------------------
#
# CHECK_BOARDID pins the module FAMILY: a P3834 (Thor) CVM is the only thing
# this carrier takes, and a module from another family fails fast at the
# tegraflash board-ID check instead of being flashed.
#
# CHECK_BOARDSKU is deliberately CLEARED. The MACHINE bakes 0008 (T5000);
# leaving it would reject the T4000 this extension also supports. The SKU is
# read off the module instead, and initrd-flash.sh refuses a P3834 SKU it
# ships no files for — so clearing this loses no protection against a
# mis-flash, it only stops pinning one of the two modules we do support.
#
# NOTE: Advantech additionally sets SKIP_EEPROM_CHECK=1 in their conf. We do
# NOT: the prod-BCT I2C1 fix above is what makes the SoM EEPROM readable in the
# first place, and the EEPROM is now what selects the module — skipping the
# read would take the auto-detection with it. If a board ever fails here with
# the EEPROM unreadable, that is a real carrier fault worth seeing.
CARRIER_FV_CHECK_BOARDID="3834"
CARRIER_FV_CHECK_BOARDSKU=""
